WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.127
This example creates a view with quality flags.

00:00:03.127 --> 00:00:08.882
It marks missing names, invalid email shapes, and invalid phone shapes.

00:00:08.882 --> 00:00:13.022
The final SELECT returns only rows that need attention.

00:00:13.022 --> 00:00:19.687
Because the source customer table is unchanged, the view becomes a transparent review layer.

00:00:19.687 --> 00:00:23.532
This is a strong pattern for reproducible cleaning.

00:00:23.532 --> 00:00:28.747
When reading the example, do not read punctuation one character at a time.

00:00:28.747 --> 00:00:33.232
Start with the purpose of the query, then identify the input rows, the operation being applied,

00:00:33.232 --> 00:00:37.727
and the shape of the expected output.

00:00:37.727 --> 00:00:42.757
In a real setting such as a healthcare pipeline validating contact, demographic, and event fields,

00:00:42.757 --> 00:00:50.042
this habit makes code reviews much easier because you can explain what the query is supposed to guarantee

00:00:50.042 --> 00:00:53.057
before discussing syntax.

00:00:53.057 --> 00:00:59.440
After running it, inspect a few records manually and check that the result matches that explanation.
